1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,520
<font color="#000000">Vision transformers are overtaking convolutional networks,</font>

2
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:09,185
<font color="#000000">but their size makes privacy-preserving distributed
training hard.</font>

3
00:00:09,265 --> 00:00:12,153
<font color="#000000">Federated learning must move whole models,</font>

4
00:00:12,233 --> 00:00:19,221
<font color="#000000">and split learning leaks privacy because a
transformer's smashed data still resembles the raw input.</font>

5
00:00:19,301 --> 00:00:23,109
<font color="#000000">From Yonsei, Deakin, M.I.T., and Oulu, at NeurIPS 2022,</font>

6
00:00:23,189 --> 00:00:25,371
<font color="#000000">this paper proposes DP-CutMixSL,</font>

7
00:00:25,451 --> 00:00:31,449
<font color="#000000">pairing a Gaussian differential-privacy mechanism
with a patch-level randomized CutMix</font>

8
00:00:31,529 --> 00:00:34,347
<font color="#000000">that mixes masked patches across clients.</font>

9
00:00:34,427 --> 00:00:37,740
<font color="#000000">It strengthens privacy while improving accuracy.</font>

10
00:00:38,120 --> 00:00:41,577
<font color="#000000">We want to train on user data without exposing it.</font>

11
00:00:41,657 --> 00:00:46,034
<font color="#000000">Federated learning keeps data local but exchanges
whole models,</font>

12
00:00:46,114 --> 00:00:48,156
<font color="#000000">costly for large transformers.</font>

13
00:00:48,236 --> 00:00:53,462
<font color="#000000">Split learning instead shares only cut-layer
activations, the smashed data.</font>

14
00:00:53,542 --> 00:00:56,858
<font color="#000000">But transformers have no pooling or convolution,</font>

15
00:00:56,938 --> 00:01:03,366
<font color="#000000">so their smashed data barely distorts the input and
stays visually similar to the raw image.</font>

16
00:01:03,446 --> 00:01:08,035
<font color="#000000">That similarity leaks privacy and, preserving so
much information,</font>

17
00:01:08,115 --> 00:01:10,370
<font color="#000000">also inflates communication cost.</font>

18
00:01:10,750 --> 00:01:15,507
<font color="#000000">As transformers displace CNNs, do the old privacy
recipes still hold?</font>

19
00:01:15,587 --> 00:01:18,311
<font color="#000000">The authors note three things about ViT.</font>

20
00:01:18,391 --> 00:01:23,148
<font color="#000000">First, without pooling its hidden representation is
barely distorted,</font>

21
00:01:23,228 --> 00:01:27,283
<font color="#000000">so regularizing it works as well as regularizing
the input,</font>

22
00:01:27,363 --> 00:01:29,667
<font color="#000000">yet high mutual information leaks.</font>

23
00:01:29,747 --> 00:01:35,485
<font color="#000000">Second, global self-attention makes ViT robust to
large noise on part of the image,</font>

24
00:01:35,565 --> 00:01:37,448
<font color="#000000">ideal for Cutout and CutMix.</font>

25
00:01:37,528 --> 00:01:40,112
<font color="#000000">Third, every operation is patch-level.</font>

26
00:01:40,192 --> 00:01:46,070
<font color="#000000">Together these point to a patch-level randomized
CutMix of the hidden representation.</font>

27
00:01:46,450 --> 00:01:48,589
<font color="#000000">Here is DP-CutMixSL step by step.</font>

28
00:01:48,669 --> 00:01:56,256
<font color="#000000">A mixer draws mixing ratios lambda from a Dirichlet
distribution and builds a pseudorandom binary mask per client,</font>

29
00:01:56,336 --> 00:01:59,821
<font color="#000000">with on-patches proportional to that client's lambda.</font>

30
00:01:59,901 --> 00:02:04,596
<font color="#000000">Each client runs its input through the lower model
to get smashed data,</font>

31
00:02:04,676 --> 00:02:06,883
<font color="#000000">masks it into Cutout smashed data,</font>

32
00:02:06,963 --> 00:02:11,860
<font color="#000000">then adds white Gaussian noise for DP-Cutout
smashed data, and uploads it.</font>

33
00:02:11,940 --> 00:02:17,778
<font color="#000000">Because the masks are mutually exclusive and
collectively exhaustive at the patch level,</font>

34
00:02:17,858 --> 00:02:24,503
<font color="#000000">the server simply adds the clients' noisy patches
into DP-CutMix smashed data with no blank patches,</font>

35
00:02:24,583 --> 00:02:27,261
<font color="#000000">mixing labels by the same lambda weights.</font>

36
00:02:27,341 --> 00:02:32,305
<font color="#000000">Forward and backward propagation then proceed as in
vanilla split learning.</font>

37
00:02:32,385 --> 00:02:37,080
<font color="#000000">Crucially, only a noisy fraction of each client's
data is ever exposed.</font>

38
00:02:37,460 --> 00:02:43,524
<font color="#000000">Without noise, CutMixSL gives the best top-1
accuracy in every configuration but one.</font>

39
00:02:43,604 --> 00:02:49,089
<font color="#000000">On CIFAR-10 with ViT-Tiny it reaches seventy-three
point seven seven percent,</font>

40
00:02:49,169 --> 00:02:54,365
<font color="#000000">versus fifty-seven for plain split learning and
sixty-eight for SplitFed.</font>

41
00:02:54,445 --> 00:02:57,835
<font color="#000000">With PiT-Tiny it hits seventy-one point two six.</font>

42
00:02:57,915 --> 00:03:03,328
<font color="#000000">On Fashion-MNIST it tops eighty-nine percent across
all three architectures.</font>

43
00:03:03,408 --> 00:03:08,893
<font color="#000000">The exception is VGG-16, where Mixup edges ahead,
because CNNs focus locally,</font>

44
00:03:08,973 --> 00:03:14,820
<font color="#000000">so replacing whole patches costs them more
information than it costs transformers.</font>

45
00:03:15,200 --> 00:03:19,113
<font color="#000000">Ablations probe the privacy-accuracy trade-off.</font>

46
00:03:19,193 --> 00:03:23,105
<font color="#000000">Sweeping noise variance,</font>

47
00:03:23,185 --> 00:03:27,098
<font color="#000000">DP-CutMixSL has the best accuracy at nearly every
level and always beats DP-MixSL.</font>

48
00:03:27,178 --> 00:03:31,091
<font color="#000000">Its RDP epsilon is tighter than DP-SL but looser
than DP-MixSL,</font>

49
00:03:31,171 --> 00:03:35,084
<font color="#000000">exactly the trade-off theory predicts,</font>

50
00:03:35,164 --> 00:03:39,076
<font color="#000000">since Mixup melts information across the whole
representation.</font>

51
00:03:39,156 --> 00:03:43,069
<font color="#000000">Larger mixing groups lower both accuracy and
epsilon, a hiding-in-the-crowd effect.</font>

52
00:03:43,149 --> 00:03:47,062
<font color="#000000">A reconstruction attack shows robustness rising
from raw data to Mixup, patch CutMix,</font>

53
00:03:47,142 --> 00:03:51,055
<font color="#000000">then Cutout.</font>

54
00:03:51,135 --> 00:03:55,047
<font color="#000000">Finally, accuracy grows as clients scale from two to ten,</font>

55
00:03:55,127 --> 00:03:59,040
<font color="#000000">and SplitFed-style averaging helps further.</font>

56
00:03:59,420 --> 00:04:01,839
<font color="#000000">The takeaway: for vision transformers,</font>

57
00:04:01,919 --> 00:04:05,653
<font color="#000000">you need not trade accuracy for privacy in split learning.</font>

58
00:04:05,733 --> 00:04:11,243
<font color="#000000">Adding Gaussian noise and mixing randomly masked
patches across clients amplifies the</font>

59
00:04:11,323 --> 00:04:16,306
<font color="#000000">differential-privacy guarantee over plain split
learning and raises accuracy,</font>

60
00:04:16,386 --> 00:04:19,660
<font color="#000000">with the gain provably bounded by a Mixup baseline.</font>

61
00:04:19,740 --> 00:04:24,592
<font color="#000000">It works because transformers use global
self-attention at the patch level,</font>

62
00:04:24,672 --> 00:04:28,604
<font color="#000000">so swapping patches costs little, though it would
hurt a CNN.</font>

63
00:04:28,684 --> 00:04:33,470
<font color="#000000">Patch-level CutMix is a privacy regularizer built
for the transformer era.</font>
